<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liliʻuokalani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lydia Liliʻu Loloku Walania Kamakaʻeha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liliʻuokalani was the only queen regnant and the last sovereign monarch of the Hawaiian Kingdom, ruling from 1891 until the monarchy was overthrown in 1893. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, she succeeded her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">David Kalākaua</a> and sought a new constitution to restore the power of the monarchy and the voting rights of the economically disenfranchised. Pro-American residents organised as the Committee of Safety overthrew her in January 1893, backed by the landing of US Marines, and after a failed uprising she was placed under house arrest and forced to abdicate in 1895. She composed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aloha-oe">Aloha ʻOe</a> and wrote the autobiography Hawaiʻi's Story by Hawaiʻi's Queen (1898).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani.jpg" alt="Liliʻuokalani" width="330" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James J. Williams / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liliuokalani,_c._1891.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
